Scabies
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Intensely pruritic nocturnal rash, Interdigital burrows, Close contacts affected

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-035
Reason for Visit: Intractable, agonizing nocturnal pruritus resulting in severe sleep deprivation, acute excoriation, and secondary family distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient and their spouse experiencing explosive, intolerable itching that is characteristically worst at night, completely preventing sleep for the past 72 hours. The patient had scratched their skin raw, causing secondary bleeding. Urgent assessment was required to definitively diagnose the highly contagious infestation, rule out severe secondary bacterial impetiginization, and initiate immediate household eradication therapy to rapidly break the severe itch cycle and prevent further transmission.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The itching is absolute torture the second I get into bed. I have scratched my arms and stomach completely raw, and now my wife has started itching too."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an intensely pruritic papular eruption that began insidiously 3 weeks ago but has escalated into an agonizing, continuous itch over the last 5 days. The pruritus characteristically worsens significantly at night, causing severe insomnia. The patient has developed extensive excoriations across the wrists, abdomen, and thighs due to relentless scratching. Crucially, the patient notes close physical contacts (the spouse) are now experiencing identical symptoms. The severe sleep deprivation has led to acute psychological distress and severe fatigue. They deny any high fever or spreading, hot, red plaques.
Functional Status: Independent, but functionally impaired due to extreme fatigue and continuous pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: High communicability within the household; severe sleep deprivation affecting work and safety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/84 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, agitated, continuously rubbing and scratching the arms and abdomen during the interview.
Physical Exam: The skin demonstrates a widespread, intensely excoriated papular eruption. The primary involvement includes the axillae, periumbilical region, and flexor wrists. Careful inspection with a dermatoscope of the interdigital web spaces of the hands reveals several classic greyish-white, thread-like burrows (2-5mm in length). Examination of the male genitalia reveals multiple highly characteristic, intensely itchy, indurated erythematous nodules on the scrotum. There is complete sparing of the head and neck. Extensive excoriations are present, but there is no thick honey-coloured crusting or localized fluctuance to indicate severe secondary bacterial impetiginization.
Environmental Observations: Patient and spouse share a bed and living spaces, confirming high transmission risk.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. OTC antihistamines have provided zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Scabies Infestation - Status: Acute, symptomatic phase
Differentials:

Atopic Dermatitis (Severe Flare) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct presence of pathognomonic burrows in the finger webs, highly specific nodular involvement of the scrotum, and the highly contagious spread to the previously unaffected spouse.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The generalized distribution favoring flexures and genitalia, the extreme nocturnal exacerbation, and the clear involvement of a close contact.

Bedbug Bites (Cimicosis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of burrows and scrotal nodules; bedbugs typically cause grouped ("breakfast, lunch, dinner") bites on exposed skin areas, not deep flexural or genital involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to extreme sleep deprivation and high communicability)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of scabies: intractable nocturnal pruritus, interdigital burrows/scrotal nodules, and an affected close contact. Triage accurately recognized the extreme sleep deprivation and severe self-excoriation as an acute crisis requiring intervention. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was critical to perform a meticulous physical exam to find the diagnostic burrows, explicitly rule out secondary staphylococcal infection of the raw skin, and, most importantly, orchestrate an immediate, simultaneous treatment plan for the entire household. Providing the pediculicide and explicit environmental decontamination instructions instantly halted further transmission and initiated the cure.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Permethrin 5% cream. Instructed application from the jawline down to the soles of the feet, paying special attention to finger webs, navel, and genitalia, leaving it on for 8-14 hours before washing off. Prescribed oral Promethazine 25mg at night for sedation and itch control.
Positive Behavioral Change: By treating every single person in the house simultaneously tonight, regardless of whether they are itching, you will completely eradicate the mites and guarantee they cannot pass the infection back to you. By understanding that the dead mites will still cause an allergic itch for up to two weeks, you will avoid over-treating your skin with toxic creams and rely on the antihistamines to let you sleep.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to confirm eradication and manage any post-scabietic pruritus or persistent nodules.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the deeply scratched areas on your stomach or arms become bright red, hot, swollen, or begin weeping thick yellow pus, or if you develop a fever, present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Eradication requires flawless execution of the simultaneous treatment plan and environmental cleaning.
Clinical Course: The Permethrin kills the mites instantly tonight. However, your immune system will continue to react to the dead mites and their waste in your skin, so the intense itching (post-scabietic itch) will persist for 2-4 weeks, slowly fading away.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not apply the Permethrin cream multiple nights in a row, as this will severely burn your skin. Monitor the open scratches for signs of bacterial infection.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A microscopic mite has burrowed into the top layer of your skin to lay eggs. Your immune system is reacting violently to the mites, causing the extreme itch. The cream will kill all the live bugs and eggs tonight, breaking the cycle immediately.
Actionable Steps: Wash all bedding, towels, and recently worn clothes in a hot wash tomorrow morning. A second application of the Permethrin cream in exactly 7 days is mandatory to kill any newly hatched eggs.

